# public_html/api/ — the native backend.
#
# <FilesMatch> because it is inherited into subdirectories and RewriteRule is
# not — the measured lesson in SERVER-LAYOUT.md. Deny by NAME.

# The credentials. config.php holds the MySQL password; store.php and the .sql
# files are includes and import fixtures, not endpoints — nothing loses by
# denying them, and seed.mysql.sql names every product and cost price.
# site-manifest.txt is a complete map of the server — every file, with its
# sha256 — written for api/file-audit.php to read from disk. Nothing should
# fetch it over HTTP, and nothing needs to: the audit page reads it as a
# file. Measured before this was added: it answered 200. Neither the root
# .htaccess (whose extension list has no .txt) nor the line below covered it.
<FilesMatch "^(config\.php|config\.example\.php|store\.php|site-manifest\.txt|.*\.sql)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# JSON answers are per-request state. Never cache an order status.
#
# EXCEPT api.php, WHICH NOW SAYS IT ITSELF — and the exclusion is the whole
# point of the FilesMatch rather than a bare Header line.
#
# Apache's `Header set` runs on the way out and REPLACES whatever PHP put
# there, so while this matched api.php no route could choose anything else:
# ?r=products is 21,773 bytes and was answered `no-store`, re-downloaded in
# full on every navigation with the query run again each time. The four
# catalogue routes now answer `no-cache, must-revalidate` with an ETag, so an
# unchanged catalogue costs a 304 and no body, and a price edited in /backends
# is still visible on the very next request — no-cache revalidates, it does not
# go stale.
#
# `Header unset` inside a <Files "api.php"> block would NOT do this: the outer
# set has already replaced PHP's header by then, so unset leaves the response
# with no Cache-Control at all, which is heuristic caching — the worst of the
# three outcomes. The rule has to not match in the first place.
#
# Nothing is weakened by the exclusion, because store_out() — the one function
# every JSON response goes through — sets `no-store` itself on the default
# path. A route has to call store_out_cacheable() on purpose to be cacheable,
# so anything added later is private until somebody decides otherwise.
#
# Every other .php here (file-audit, cron-*, invoice-*, orders-print, webpush)
# keeps the blanket, which is why this stays a deny-by-default rule with one
# named hole rather than a list of the files it should cover.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "^(?!api\.php$).*$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-store"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

Options -Indexes
